1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,699
Friday afternoon.

2
00:00:01,899 --> 00:00:04,458
Morgan runs a small community learning program.

3
00:00:04,458 --> 00:00:10,075
For this course, we’ll follow Harbor Learning Circle, an illustrative case built from fictional records.

4
00:00:10,075 --> 00:00:13,633
Someone suggests adding a free digital-skills session next month.

5
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:20,272
Three notes are on the table: an attendance count, a facilitator availability note, and a room-capacity note.

6
00:00:21,072 --> 00:00:25,169
An assistant can turn those into a neat recommendation in seconds.

7
00:00:25,969 --> 00:00:28,603
But if Morgan asks, ‘Where did this come from?’

8
00:00:29,403 --> 00:00:31,795
a polished paragraph is thin protection.

9
00:00:32,595 --> 00:00:33,785
The work needs a trail.

10
00:00:34,585 --> 00:00:39,180
Our job today is small: design one preparation role and one receipt.

11
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:42,921
The agent does not decide whether the session happens.

12
00:00:43,721 --> 00:00:46,678
It prepares a Session Option Brief for Morgan to review.

13
00:00:47,478 --> 00:00:49,094
That distinction matters.

14
00:00:49,894 --> 00:00:55,569
If we give the system a loose command like ‘run the workshop program,’ we have no useful boundary.

15
00:00:56,369 --> 00:00:58,381
What exactly begins its work?

16
00:00:59,181 --> 00:01:00,393
What can it read?

17
00:01:01,193 --> 00:01:02,835
What must it hand back?

18
00:01:03,635 --> 00:01:05,118
Who owns the outcome?

19
00:01:05,918 --> 00:01:08,496
These are ordinary management questions.

20
00:01:09,296 --> 00:01:13,397
They become more urgent when a tool can produce work while nobody is watching.

21
00:01:14,197 --> 00:01:23,794
Write the job in one sentence: ‘When Morgan requests a monthly session review, prepare one brief comparing a proposed free session against the three cleared inputs.’

22
00:01:24,594 --> 00:01:26,861
The trigger is Morgan's request.

23
00:01:27,661 --> 00:01:29,885
The output is a draft brief.

24
00:01:30,685 --> 00:01:31,579
The manager is Morgan.

25
00:01:32,379 --> 00:01:40,158
The measure is whether Morgan can check the claim and source for every recommendation without chasing the agent through a log.

26
00:01:40,958 --> 00:01:47,386
The forbidden actions are just as plain: no booking, no announcement, no message to participants, no spending.

27
00:01:48,186 --> 00:01:53,558
A useful agent job names the work it finishes and the work it leaves alone.

28
00:01:54,358 --> 00:01:56,264
Now attach the receipt.

29
00:01:57,064 --> 00:02:00,204
Imagine the brief says the room can hold twenty people.

30
00:02:01,004 --> 00:02:09,458
Morgan needs to see which note supplied that number, when the brief was prepared, what the agent actually did, and whether anyone approved it.

31
00:02:10,258 --> 00:02:15,976
The receipt also records cost or resource use, outcome, and how to pull the draft back.

32
00:02:16,776 --> 00:02:18,685
A blank field stays blank and gets an owner.

33
00:02:19,485 --> 00:02:23,299
We never fill a gap with a confident guess.

34
00:02:24,099 --> 00:02:29,701
The first receipt is a handoff a busy person can understand; the engineering log can remain behind it.

35
00:02:30,501 --> 00:02:33,756
In our case, approval is ‘pending Morgan.’

36
00:02:34,556 --> 00:02:36,950
The outcome is ‘draft prepared, no session selected.’

37
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:44,589
The rollback is ‘withdraw the draft from the review folder and mark it superseded.’

38
00:02:45,389 --> 00:02:49,381
If the source for room capacity is missing, mark the brief blocked.

39
00:02:50,181 --> 00:02:56,994
A complete-looking card without a source can move bad information faster than an obviously unfinished one.

40
00:02:57,794 --> 00:03:02,259
The receipt makes that gap visible before anyone treats the draft as a decision.

41
00:03:03,059 --> 00:03:04,076
Take a moment.

42
00:03:04,885 --> 00:03:07,412
Picture one workflow in your own work.

43
00:03:08,221 --> 00:03:11,356
Keep this exercise on paper or in a local draft.

44
00:03:12,165 --> 00:03:14,678
Use a fictional or approved example.

45
00:03:15,487 --> 00:03:18,035
Do not use private operational data.

46
00:03:18,844 --> 00:03:22,250
What is the one useful output, and who would have to defend it?

47
00:03:23,059 --> 00:03:25,537
Fill the Job and Receipt boxes.

48
00:03:25,787 --> 00:03:30,439
Name the trigger, permitted inputs, output, human manager, success check, and three forbidden actions.

49
00:03:30,689 --> 00:03:33,918
Then fill the nine receipt fields for one imaginary run.

50
00:03:34,168 --> 00:03:42,123
If you cannot name a source, approval state, or rollback, write ‘unknown’ and the person who must resolve it.

51
00:03:42,373 --> 00:03:45,299
Do not open a real system to complete this exercise.

52
00:03:45,549 --> 00:03:52,604
The point is to see the shape of the work before asking a tool to do it.

53
00:05:43,059 --> 00:05:46,984
Save those two boxes in your Agentified Operating File as a draft.

54
00:05:48,879 --> 00:05:55,004
Your stop condition is simple: if the owner, source, or review gate is missing, the draft stays on paper.

55
00:05:56,899 --> 00:05:59,645
Next we will name the decision Morgan actually owns.
